/T (For patients who are haemodynamically stable and have no vomiting or diarrhoea, increased oral fluid intake may be sufficient during the critical phase. However, IV fluid is indicated in patients with rising HCT \(indicating on-going plasma leakage\) despite increased oral intake. IV fluid therapy should also be considered in patients who are vomiting and not tolerating oral intake.)
/E (For patients who are haemodynamically stable and have no vomiting or diarrhoea, increased oral fluid intake may be sufficient during the critical phase. However, IV fluid is indicated in patients with rising HCT \(indicating on-going plasma leakage\) despite increased oral intake. IV fluid therapy should also be considered in patients who are vomiting and not tolerating oral intake.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Considering the dynamic nature of plasma leakage, fluids should not be given at a flat rate. In the early stages of the critical phase, the degree of leakage is not predictable. Therefore, after detecting the critical phase, the fluid rate should be increased to 2.5 to 3 ml/kg/ hour \(maximum 50 kg\) for patients in the first half of the critical phase. After 3-4 hours, fluid rate should be adjusted according to the UOP, haemodynamics and the HCT.  )
/E (Considering the dynamic nature of plasma leakage, fluids should not be given at a flat rate. In the early stages of the critical phase, the degree of leakage is not predictable. Therefore, after detecting the critical phase, the fluid rate should be increased to 2.5 to 3 ml/kg/ hour \(maximum 50 kg\) for patients in the first half of the critical phase. After 3-4 hours, fluid rate should be adjusted according to the UOP, haemodynamics and the HCT.  )
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [14 15]
>>
endobj
1596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Similarly, when plasma leakage becomes less \(second half of the critical phase\) it is necessary to reduce the rate of fluid intake in a step-wise pattern. Urine output should be maintained at 0.5-1 ml/kg/hour \(Calculate the urine output in ml/kg/hour, using the same weight used for fluid calculation.\) and pulse pressure above 30 mmHg during the entire critical period.)
/E (Similarly, when plasma leakage becomes less \(second half of the critical phase\) it is necessary to reduce the rate of fluid intake in a step-wise pattern. Urine output should be maintained at 0.5-1 ml/kg/hour \(Calculate the urine output in ml/kg/hour, using the same weight used for fluid calculation.\) and pulse pressure above 30 mmHg during the entire critical period.)
/Pg 50 0 R
>>
endobj
