1614 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (If the patient has signs of impending shock \(Table 4.1 - Detection of compensated/ decompensated and profound shock in dengue\) rate of fluid should be increased to 5-7 ml/kg/hour to keep the vital parameters stable. However, a rate as high as 5-7 ml/kg/hour is only occasionally needed and will be necessary only for a few hours. If the patient improves, fluid can be gradually tailed off. If the patient does not improve, consider giving more fluid, blood or dextran depending on the HCT and the available fluid quota.)
/E (If the patient has signs of impending shock \(Table 4.1 - Detection of compensated/ decompensated and profound shock in dengue\) rate of fluid should be increased to 5-7 ml/kg/hour to keep the vital parameters stable. However, a rate as high as 5-7 ml/kg/hour is only occasionally needed and will be necessary only for a few hours. If the patient improves, fluid can be gradually tailed off. If the patient does not improve, consider giving more fluid, blood or dextran depending on the HCT and the available fluid quota.)
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
