/T (If the patient is haemodynamically stable and has a normal or low HCT, fluid should be restricted and the patient should be monitored carefully, as the patient is likely to improve within hours. The most probable reason for low haematocrit is haemodilution. However, consider the possibility of concealed bleeding and reserve blood to transfuse if the patient becomes unstable. If the patient develops features of pulmonary oedema, furosemide 5-10 mg should be given intravenously. This dose can be repeated after half an hour.)
/E (If the patient is haemodynamically stable and has a normal or low HCT, fluid should be restricted and the patient should be monitored carefully, as the patient is likely to improve within hours. The most probable reason for low haematocrit is haemodilution. However, consider the possibility of concealed bleeding and reserve blood to transfuse if the patient becomes unstable. If the patient develops features of pulmonary oedema, furosemide 5-10 mg should be given intravenously. This dose can be repeated after half an hour.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [12]
>>
